Sure! The Bubble Sort algorithm is a simple sorting algorithm that repeatedly steps through the list to be sorted, compares each pair of adjacent items and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the list is repeated until no swaps are needed, which indicates that the list is sorted.
In the DisplayAll panel, you should use the Bubble Sort algorithm to sort the array of Student objects based on their names, in ascending order. To do this, you can implement a method that iterates through the array multiple times, comparing adjacent Student objects and swapping them if necessary. The comparisons should be based on the student names (i.e. LastName property) of the Student objects.
Here's some sample code for implementing the Bubble Sort algorithm to sort an array of Student objects in ascending order based on their names:
```
public void bubbleSort() {
int n = students.length;
boolean swapped = true;
while (swapped) {
swapped = false;
for (int I = 1; I < n; i++) {
if (students[i - 1].getLastName().compareTo(students[i].getLastName()) > 0) {
// swap students[i-1] and students[i]
Student temp = students[i - 1];
students[i - 1] = students[i];
students[i] = temp;
swapped = true;
}
}
n--;
}
}
```
In this example, `students` is the array of Student objects, and `getLastName()` is a method in the Student class that returns the last name of the student as a String. The `compareTo()` method is used to compare the last names of adjacent Student objects, and the `swapped` boolean variable is used to track whether any swaps were made in a particular iteration of the loop. The `n` variable is used to keep track of the end of the current unsorted portion of the array, and is decremented each time the loop completes a full iteration. Once the loop completes without any swaps, the array is sorted.

