Here's an implementation of bubble sort in JavaScript that arranges an array of numbers in ascending order:
```
function bubbleSort(arr) {
// Loop through the array from start to end
for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
// Loop through the array from start to i-th element from end
for (let j = 0; j < (arr.length - I - 1); j++) {
// Swap if the element found is greater than the next element
if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
const tmp = arr[j];
arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
arr[j + 1] = tmp;
}
}
}
// Return the sorted array
return arr;
}
// Example usage
const unsortedArray = [6, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3];
const sortedArray = bubbleSort(unsortedArray);
console.log(sortedArray); // prints [1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 9]
```
The `bubbleSort` function takes an array `arr` as input, and loops through the array from start to end using the outer loop. In the inner loop, it loops through the array from start to the `i`-th element from the end, and compares each element with the next element using an `if` statement. If the element is greater than the next element, they are swapped using a temporary variable `tmp`.
After looping through the array, the sorted array is returned from the function.
I've added comments to explain each part of the code, and followed best coding practices by using `const` and `let` to declare variables, using meaningful variable and function names, and using braces to group statements in control structures like `if` statements and loops.

