```java
public class CountingSort {
public static void main(String[] args) {
int[] arr = {5, 2, 9, 5, 2, 3, 7, 4, 1, 0, -3, -2, 6, 9};
countingSort(arr);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
}
public static void countingSort(int[] arr) {
if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) return;
// Find range of input array
int min = arr[0];
int max = arr[0];
for (int I = 1; I < arr.length; i++) {
if (arr[i] < min) {
min = arr[i];
} else if (arr[i] > max) {
max = arr[i];
}
}
// Initialize count array and output array
int[] count = new int[max - min + 1];
int[] output = new int[arr.length];
// Count occurrences of each number in input array
for (int I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
count[arr[i] - min]++;
}
// Sum up counts to obtain indices for output array
for (int I = 1; I < count.length; i++) {
count[i] += count[i - 1];
}
// Build output array
for (int I = arr.length - 1; I >= 0; i--) {
output[--count[arr[i] - min]] = arr[i];
}
// Copy output array back to input array
System.arraycopy(output, 0, arr, 0, arr.length);
}
}
```
The counting sort implementation first finds the range of the input array by iterating through it. It then initializes a count array and an output array, both of which are the same length as the input array. The count array stores the occurrences of each number in the input array, while the output array will store the sorted output.
The algorithm counts the occurrences of each number in the input array by incrementing the corresponding count array entry each time the number is encountered.
The algorithm then computes the indices for the output array by summing up the counts in the count array.
Finally, the algorithm builds the output array by iterating backwards through the input array and placing each element in its correct position in the output array using the indices in the count array.
The output array is then copied back to the input array to complete the sorting process.

