Now, let's discuss the different types of animation timing functions and how they affect the animation.
## Types of Animation Timing Functions
Animation timing functions control the acceleration and deceleration of an animation. There are several types of timing functions, including:
### ease (default)
The default timing function is "ease", which starts slow, accelerates quickly, then slows down again towards the end of the animation. This is a good all-purpose timing function that creates a gentle start and finish to the animation.
### linear
The "linear" timing function creates a constant rate of change throughout the animation, with no acceleration or deceleration.
### ease-in
The "ease-in" timing function starts slow and gradually increases in speed towards the end of the animation, creating a feeling of acceleration.
### ease-out
The "ease-out" timing function starts fast and gradually slows down towards the end of the animation, creating a feeling of deceleration.
### ease-in-out
The "ease-in-out" timing function combines elements of the "ease-in" and "ease-out" functions, starting slow, accelerating in the middle, and slowing back down towards the end.
By experimenting with different timing functions, you can create different effects such as a slower or more dramatic start, or more abrupt or gradual deceleration.

